      "problem": "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14268503/assets/33b75377650ada38e596de266f364861c62149c1278e979127cc5e7bbcbc4694.svg) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14268503/assets/c8488617fa8b97b1a2a138c845ddc613b6daefbdb0c7e1d431da7f7957e81f0c.svg) are orthogonal vectors, find the value of $k.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-4)+(-3)\\cdot 6+k\\cdot (-6)+(-2)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-20-18-6k-16 \\\\  & =-6k-54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the last line to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6k-54 & =0 \\\\ -6k & =54 \\\\ k & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
